# Poly(3‐hexylthiophene)/perovskite Heterointerface by Spinodal Decomposition Enabling Efficient and Stable Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
The best research-cell efficiency of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) is comparable with that of mature silicon solar cells (SSCs); However, the industrial development of PSCs lags far behind SSCs. PSC is a multiphase and multicomponent system, whose consequent interfacial energy loss and carrier loss seriously affect the performance and stability of devices. Here, by using spinodal decomposition, a spontaneous solid phase segregation process, in situ introduces a poly(3-hexylthiophene)/perovskite (P3HT/PVK) heterointerface with interpenetrating structure in PSCs. The P3HT/PVK heterointerface tunes the energy alignment, thereby reducing the energy loss at the interface; The P3HT/PVK interpenetrating structure bridges a transport channel, thus decreasing the carrier loss at the interface. The simultaneous mitigation of energy and carrier losses by P3HT/PVK heterointerface enables n-i-p geometry device a power conversion efficiency of 24.53% (certified 23.94%) and excellent stability. These findings demonstrate an ingenious strategy to optimize the performance of PSCs by heterointerface via Spinodal decomposition.

The best research-cell efficiency of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) is comparable with that of mature silicon solar cells (SSCs); However, the industrial development of PSCs lags far behind SSCs. PSC is a multiphase and multicomponent system, whose consequent interfacial energy loss and carrier loss seriously affect the performance and stability of devices. Here, by using spinodal decomposition, a spontaneous solid phase segregation process, in situ introduces a poly(3-hexylthiophene)/perovskite (P3HT/PVK) heterointerface with interpenetrating structure in PSCs. The P3HT/PVK heterointerface tunes the energy alignment, thereby reducing the energy loss at the interface; The P3HT/PVK interpenetrating structure bridges a transport channel, thus decreasing the carrier loss at the interface. The simultaneous mitigation of energy and carrier losses by P3HT/PVK heterointerface enables n-i-p geometry device a power conversion efficiency of $24.53\%$ (certified $23.94\%$ and excellent stability. These findings demonstrate an ingenious strategy to optimize the performance of PSCs by heterointerface via Spinodal decomposition.
## 1. Introduction
Lead halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have recently achieved a certified power conversion efficiency of $26.0\%$ , very close to the best research-cell efficiency $(26.1\%)$ of mature silicon solar cells (SSCs).[1] However, the practical application of PSCs lags far behind SSCs and still faces the challenge of improving performance and stability.[2,3] PSC is a multiphase and multicomponent system, the consequent interfaces highly affect charge carriers’ generation, extraction, transportation, and recombination.[4–6] The energy level mismatch at the interface causes energy loss and poor carrier extraction, and the interface defects limit carrier transportation and induce carrier nonradiative recombination.[7,8] To alleviate the interface loss, various approaches have been attempted, such as reducing defects by controlling growth conditions, passivating defects by additives, optimizing energy alignment by doping, self-assembled monolayer, two-dimensional/three-dimensional (2D/3D) heterojunction, etc.[7–10] Despite appreciable progress, it is still difficult to solve the energy loss and carrier loss at the interface by traditional paths.
Different from current strategies, we design an intelligent strategy of spinodal decomposition to in situ introduce a poly(3- hexylthiophene)/perovskite (P3HT/PVK) heterointerface with an interpenetrating network structure to simultaneously alleviate the defects and optimize the energy alignment at the interface of perovskite layer/hole transport layer (HTL). P3HT is an organic p-type semiconductor with a high hole mobility of $0.1\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , and its HOMO energy level $(-5.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ is close to that of spiroOMeTAD $(-5.22\mathrm{eV})$ .[4,11,12] P3HT is an appropriative intermediary material between PVK and HTL.[13] However, P3HT as an HTL often results in lower open circuit voltage of devices.[14] This is attributed to the poor physical contact between P3HT and PVK, which impedes efficient carrier transport.[15] Fortunately, spinodal decomposition can effectively avoid the dilemma. Spinodal decomposition, or metastable decomposition,[16,17] is a spontaneous solid phase segregation process, which not only in suit generates two phases with bi-continuous and interpenetrating network structure, but also renders the intricate control of nucleation processes unnecessary. The simultaneous spontaneous in situ formation of PVK and P3HT phases solves the problem of poor interface contact and many grain boundary defects between PVK and P3HT; Second, the gradual precipitation of P3HT phase helps to construct gradient materials with gradually changing P3HT concentration, reducing the energy barrier for hole transport through P3HT/PVK heterointerface. Therefore, in the photovoltaic conversion process of PSCs ,both carrier loss and energy loss can be effectively reduced.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Formation of P3HT/PVK Heterointerface
In this study, dimethyl sulfoxide/dimethylformamide (DMSO/DMF) and chlorobenzene (CB) are chosen as the solvent and antisolvent of perovskite. These solvent displays the semi-orthogonal nature of the P3HT and PVK as shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information). When P3HT/CB solution was dropped into perovskite precursor DMSO-DMF solution, the perovskite precursor solution was extracted by CB anti-solvent to form a solid PVK phase, and the blend of remaining PVK and P3HT was in an unstable state, i.e., spinodal zone.[18,19] To reduce the Gibbs free energy (∆G) of the system, uphill diffusion (Spinodal decomposition) occurred spontaneously. P3HT molecules gathered to form P3HT-rich phase, meanwhile PVK molecules aggregated to form the PVK-rich phase. Since the PVK and P3HT phases formed at the same time and in the same space, the two phases intertwined each other to form an interpenetrating network structure.[20,21] A similar process was confirmed in the preparation of P3HT/PCBM interpenetrating networks through spinodal decomposition and chlorobenzene solvent evaporation.[22] The in situ preparation of P3HT/PVK heterointerface and the spinodal decomposition are schematically illustrated in Figure 1a,b. XRD patterns shown in Figure 1c verify the coexistence of PVK and P3HT phases[23] when the P3HT concentration is higher than $1.0\ \mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ . The observed shift in peak position with the concentration increases suggests that the molecular arrangement and packing of P3HT chains are affected by the interaction between P3HT and PVK.
Based on the interaction model shown in Figure 1d, the density functional theory (DFT) calculation was implemented,[24–26] and the detail results are presented in Note S1 (Supporting Information). Figure S2 (Supporting Information) displays the relationship between the Gibbs free energy, and the first and second derivatives of the Gibbs free energy with the volume fraction of P3HT (x), respectively. Based on the calculation, the phase diagram of the P3HT/PVK blend can be deduced in Figure 1e. Where the red line represents the spinodal line, and the blue one is a binodal line, respectively. The red region is thermodynamically unstable region., i.e., When the volume fraction of P3HT is in the range of $0.085\mathrm{~-~}0.915$ and the temperature is 381K, the spinodal decomposition of the P3HT/PVK blend will occur spontaneously. In our experiments, the temperature was controlled at $381~\mathrm{K},$ and the concentration of P3HT was optimized to be $2.0~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ (volume fraction ${\approx}0.094)$ , which had just fallen in the spinodal decomposition zone. Theoretical calculation demonstrates the feasibility of the spinodal decomposition of the P3HT/PVK blend in our experiments.
## 2.2. Morphological and Compositional Analysis
Figure 2a,b and Figure S3 (Supporting Information) compare the cross-view scan electron microscopy (SEM) images of the devices with varying concentrations of P3HT. The P3HT/PVK device $(2.0~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ ) shows larger perovskite grains, which is mainly attributed to the uphill diffusion of perovskite components driven by spinodal decomposition. Figure S4 (Supporting Information) shows top-view Transmission electron microscope (TEM) images of P3HT/PVK with elemental analyses of lead (Pb) and sulfur (S). In the upper layer, reduced Pb content from the perovskite and higher S content mainly from P3HT suggest the P3HT/PVK heterointerface. This region is outlined by red dashed lines in the TEM images, with an estimated depth of ${\approx}30~\mathrm{nm}$ . The top-view SEM images of the perovskite films with different content of P3HT are compared in Figure 2c,d and Figure S5 (Supporting Information). The P3HT/PVK films display highly interconnected and continuous structures instead of scattered dots. Interestingly, the interconnected area of the specimen increases with increasing P3HT concentration, which seems to be consistent with the morphological characteristics of spinodal decomposition rather than the nucleation growth mechanism.[27]
Figure S6b (Supporting Information) presents the Raman spectra of PVK and P3HT/PVK. For PVK, the peak at $543~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is ascribed to NCN from perovskite[31]; in P3HT/PVK, the peak $\approx1450\:\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is associated with $\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{C}$ from P3HT.[32] The Raman intensity mappings of NCN and $\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{C}$ were used to examine the spatial distribution of PVK and P3HT. In Figure 2i, the NCN-rich (PVK-rich) region is on the bottom left area; in Figure 2j, the ${\sf C}=$ C-rich (P3HT-rich) region in on the top right area. These observations suggest the presence of interconnected P3HT-enriched regions, indicating that P3HT experiences spinodal decomposition and becomes enriched within the perovskite film at the micronano scale. Figure S6c,d (Supporting Information) show C1s and Pb 4f spectra of high-resolution X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) of PVK and P3HT/PVK films. The P3HT/PVK has an additional peak at $285.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ in C 1s spectra, which is assigned to the C–S bond from P3HT. The two main peaks at 142.6 and $137.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ of Pb 4f shift up by $0.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ after P3HT incorporation, suggesting that the sulfur element on P3HT bond with the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ in perovskite,[33] which lead to the passivation of defects. The time of flight secondary ion mass spectrometry (ToF-SIMS) depth profile in Figure S7 (Supporting Information) illustrates that ${\sf S}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{{\calSH}^{-}}$ , and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{SH}^{-}$ are predominantly situated in the upper region of the P3HT/PVK film. Examining Table S1 (Supporting Information), which represents the signal of pure P3HT, confirms that the presence of $S^{-}$ , $\mathrm{{\calSH}^{-}}$ , and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{SH}^{-}$ in P3HT/PVK primarily stems from P3HT. Furthermore, the slower etching pattern in Figure S8 (Supporting Information) provides additional insight, indicating that in P3HT/PVK, P3HT is primarily distributed in the upper layer, consistent with the results of TEM in Figure S4 (Supporting Information). These observations fully verify the $\mathrm{{\cal{S}}H_{\mu}^{-}}$ from the P3HT that is mainly distributed on the upper part of the P3HT/PVK films. This may be because in a mixed system of PVK and P3HT, PVK with a higher concentration first crystallizes, while P3HT with a lower concentration gradually crystallizes later, resulting in a higher concentration of P3HT in the upper part of P3HT/PVK.[27] The formation of gradient material is profitable to carrier transport with a lower energy barrier.
## 2.3. Energy Alignment
The energy level alignment at the heterointerface greatly affect the energy and carrier loss of the devices.[34] Based on Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) technique, Figure 3a–d show the topography map and surface potential map of the PVK and P3HT/PVK films. The P3HT/PVK exhibits lower average contact potential difference (CPD) values $(-71.4~\mathrm{mV})$ than that of PVK $(-26.6\:\mathrm{mV})$ . The potential of P3HT/PVK shows a more significant variation at line profiles than that of PVK (Figure 3e,f), since the value mainly depends on the composition variation. Besides, the average surface potential of P3HT/PVK in the vertical distance (Figure S9, Supporting Information) is cyclic alternation with significant change amplitudes. P3HT/PVK exhibits a broader potential distribution, as depicted in Figure 3f (right), despite a narrower height distribution in Figure 3f (left). This phenomenon can be attributed to the component distribution within
Using a monochromatic He-I𝛼 with an energy of $21.22\ \mathrm{eV}$ as light source, ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) of samples were measured and shown in Figure 3g. Based on the UV–Vis absorption spectra, the Tauc plot of PVK and P3HT/PVK can be drawn and shown in Figure S10 (Supporting Information). Combined UPS spectra with and Tauc plot, the corresponding energy levels were calculated[35] and shown in Figure 3h and Table S2 (Supporting Information). P3HT/PVK shows larger work functions (WFs) of $4.92\ \mathrm{eV}$ compared with that of PVK of $4.66\:\mathrm{eV_{\it\mathrm{i}}}$ in excellent agreement with the lower surface potential in KPFM, which drives the holes to extract from perovskite.[36] The HOMO of P3HT/PVK $(-5.60\mathrm{eV})$ has a more match energy level to HTL (spiro-OMeTAD, $-5.22\ \mathrm{eV})^{[4]}$ with an energy offset of $0.38\mathrm{eV}$ than that of the pristine PVK $(-5.70~\mathrm{eV})$ with energy offset of $0.48\ \mathrm{eV}.$ The P3HT/PVK heterointerface optimizes the interface energy alignment between perovskite and HTL, and thus reduces the energy loss at the interface.[37]
## 2.4. Carrier Transport Dynamics
The photoluminescence (PL) maps are measured and shown in Figure 4a,b, Figures S11a–c and S12 (Supporting Information) to investigate the dependence of spatial distribution of the photoelectronic properties on the concentrations of P3HT. With P3HT concentration increases, a red shift in the peak position is observed (Figure S11a–c, Supporting Information), and the corresponding PL area associated with red-shift regions also exhibits an increasing trend. Figure 4a,b, along with the corresponding energy histograms in Figure S12 (Supporting Information), demonstrate a wider energy distribution in P3HT/PVK compared to PVK. This distribution can be divided into two regions. The low-energy part, corresponding to longer wavelengths, is attributed to the P3HT-rich region, while the other part is associated with the PVK-rich region, indicating the interaction between PVK and P3HT molecules.[38] Additionally, after the P3HT modification the red shift of PL spectra and the corresponding UV–Vis absorption spectra is consistent (Figure S13a, Supporting Information), which aligns well with the observation from the PL mapping. The red shift is mainly attributed to the introduction of the P3HT/PVK heterointerface, facilitating hole extraction from the perovskite due to the p-type nature of P3HT.[39] Furthermore, when the spiro-OMeTAD (HTL) is deposited on the P3HT/PVK films (Figure S13b, Supporting Information), a significant decrease in the PL peak intensity of the P3HT/PVK film is observed. The reduced PL intensity is attributed to the effective hole extraction and transportation.[38,40]
The time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) was measured and corresponding decays (Figure 4c) were fitted by a biexponential rate law (Note S2, Supporting Information),[41] and the results are shown in Table S3 (Supporting Information). P3HT/PVK film exhibit fast decays $\langle\tau_{1}=3.71$ ns, $\tau_{2}=21.75$ ns) compared to that of PVK film $\prime_{\tau_{1}}=13.95\mathrm{ns}$ , $\tau_{2}=101.96\mathrm{ns}_{\cdot}$ , indicating an effective transport channel by ${\mathrm{P}}3{\mathrm{HT}}$ to HTL. Time-resolved surface photovoltage measurement (TR-SPV, Figure S14, Supporting Information) was utilized to evaluate the hole extract and transfer of the film. P3HT/PVK displays a prominent positive signal in SPV, rising rapidly to $31.9\mathrm{mV}$ within $0.1\upmu\mathrm{s}$ , while PVK shows limited selectivity with a $5.5\mathrm{mV}$ signal. This suggests efficient hole extract and transfer in P3HT/PVK.[42] The SPV on P3HT/PVK+HTL film shows a higher SPV peak and faster rise than the $\mathrm{PVK+HTL}$ film, a more effective hole extraction. This enhancement is consistent for both P3HT/PVK without and with HTL, suggesting no barriers between layers and affirming that P3HT treatment improves hole extraction. The charge carrier transport in PVK and P3HT/PVK is illustrated in Figure 4d. The carrier loss is inevitable during the transport process due to recombination, as shown in the PVK. For the P3HT/PVK, the p-type P3HT-rich region can penetrate the perovskite bulk through Spinodal decomposition, facilitating the separation of holes and electrons and the transportation of holes from perovskite to HTL, thus reducing carrier recombination loss.
The space charge limited current (SCLC) curves of hole-only devices are shown in Figure S15 (Supporting Information). Based on the trap-filling limited voltage $(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ from the curves, the trapstate density (Nt) of the PVK and P3HT/PVK-based devices can be calculated (Note S3, Supporting Information)[41] as $6.7\times10^{8}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $3.2\times10^{8}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. The smaller trap-state density may be due to the introduction of P3HT set up an effective hole transport channel from PVK to HTL. Figure S16 (Supporting Information) compares the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) spectra of PVK and P3HT/PVK based-devices fitted with an equivalent circuit model, and the corresponding data are listed in Table S4 (Supporting Information). The PVK/P3TH device exhibits smaller series resistance $(\mathrm{R_{S}})$ , reduced ${\bf R}_{1}$ (bulkrelated resistance and charge-transfer resistance), and elevated ${\bf R}_{2}$ (interface-related resistance and recombination resistance) compared to the pristine PVK device,[43,44] indicating that the incorporation of P3HT enhances the carrier transport and restrains non-radiative recombination.
The charge dynamic process is explored through transient photovoltage decay (TPV) and transient photocurrent decay (TPC). Charge recombination time $(\tau_{r})$ of the P3HT/PVK device $(19.63~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ is significantly longer than the pristine one $(9.73~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ via TPV analysis (Figure 4e), while charge transport time $(\tau_{t})$ is decreased from 3.50 to $3.00~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ after P3HT incorporation via TPC analysis (Figure S17, Supporting Information). The slower charge recombination and faster charge transport are attributed to the more matched energy level of PVK and HTL, less charge carrier accumulation at the interface and an effective transport channel from PVK to HTL.[45,46]
The Motty-Schottky plots in Figure $4\mathrm{f}$ show that builtin voltage $(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{bi}})$ increases from $0.97\mathrm{~V~}$ for pristine PVK to $1.12\mathrm{V}$ for P3HT/PVK heterointerface, reflecting efficient carrier transport.[47] Figure S18 (Supporting Information) provides the relationship between illumination intensity and short-circuit current density $(\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of the device based on PVK and P3HT/PVK. According to the power law: $J_{S C}\propto I^{\alpha}$ , the $\alpha$ values can be estimated to be 0.976 and 0.999 for the PVK and P3HT/PVK baseddevices, respectively, an indication that the P3HT/PVK device has less bimolecular recombination.[48] $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ versus illumination intensity (ln I) of the devices shown in Figure S19 (Supporting Information). The ideal factor (n) values of the P3HT/PVK device can be calculated (Note S4, Supporting Information)[49] as 1.01 $\mathtt{K T/q}$ , and the PVK device $\mathrm{{(n=1.20~KT/q)}}$ ), the smaller n value for P3HT/PVK device demonstrates that the trap-assisted nonradiative recombination is suppressed.[50]
## 2.5. Device Performance and Stability
Planar PSCs with configuration of $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ /CsFAMAperovskite/spiro-OMeTAD/Au were fabricated. The current density-voltage (J-V) characteristics were measured under simulated solar illumination with an intensity of $100\mathrm{\mw\cm^{-2}}$ (AM 1.5G), the results are shown in Figure 5a; Figure S20 and Table S5 (Supporting Information). When the concentration of P3HT was optimized as $2.0\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , the PSC achieved the best performance with power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $23.02\%$ , with an open-circuit voltage $(\mathrm{V_{OC}})$ of $1.214\mathrm{~V},$ a short-circuit current density $(\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of $24.29\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and a fill factor (FF) of $78.05\%$ . For the pristine device, the PCE is mediocre $20.34\%$ . While the device with high-concentrations of P3HT show poor performance. The excess P3HT does not dissolve in the precursor solution and partake in spinodal decomposition, while it is agglomerated at the interface, leading to the asymmetric transport of carriers.[51]
Based on the J-V curves in different scan directions (Figure 5a), the hysteresis index (HI) in Note S5 (Supporting Information) was calculated[52] and listed in Table S6 (Supporting Information). In contrast to the pristine PVK device with a large HI of 0.162, the P3HT/PVK device shows negligible hysteresis ( $\mathrm{~HI=~}$ 0.004). Dramatic reduction of hysteresis is probably attributed to reducing charge calculation from effective carrier transport through P3HT/PVK heterointerface.[53]
According to the incident photon-to-electron conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra (Figure S21, Supporting Information), $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J}}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ integration values from the IPCE were calculated to be 22.6 and $23.02\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for the PVK and P3HT/PVK devices, in line with the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J}}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ values from the J-V test. From Figure 5b, the P3HT/PVK device shows a larger stabilized power output (SPO) of $22.28\%$ than its counterpart $(18.45\%)$ . The statistical PCEs of 20 devices compare the reproducibility in Figure S22 (Supporting Information). After P3HT/PVK treatment, the device exhibits a better average PCE of $22.11\%$ (PVK device with $18.97\%)$ and narrower distribution, demonstrating excellent reproducibility.
Environmental stability is assessed by tracking the performance of unencapsulated devices at ambient temperature at relative humidity (RH) of $30\%$ in the dark.[54] From Figure 5c, P3HT/PVK device keeps $88.46\%$ of the initial PCE after $960\mathrm{~h~}$ while the PVK device’s efficiency is decreased to the original $45.45\%$ . The water contact angles of the films are $58.96^{\circ}$ and $96.73^{\circ}$ for PVK and P3HT/PVK films (inset), respectively. Since P3HT shows high hydrophobicity, the increase of contact angle for P3HT/PVK indicates that the P3HT enhances the humidity stability of PSCs.[46]
The thermal stability of unsealed PSCs was performed under $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ heating in an $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere.[55] The P3HT/PVKbased device maintained $73.4\%$ of the initial PCE value after $600\mathrm{~h~}$ of ageing when the pristine one was almost ineffective (Figure S23, Supporting Information), indicating excellent thermal stability. The enhancement of thermal stability of the device with P3HT/PVK heterointerface is attributed to the better thermal stability of P3HT than spiro-OMeTAD. P3HT/PVK heterointerface play an important hole transport function. At higher temperature of $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , hole transport function by Spiro is damaged, but the function of P3HT still is maintained, and the P3HT/PVK treated device shows the better thermal stability. Similar phenomenon also was found in the spiro-OMeTAD modified with ionic coupling positive polymer radicals and molecular anions through an ion-exchange process.[56] For operational stability, the maximum power point (MPP) of unencapsulated devices was systematically monitored at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a nitrogen atmosphere, employing a white light-emitting diode (LED) lamp for illumination (Figure S24, Supporting Information). The pristine device experiences a swift decline, exhibiting a rapid drop in efficiency to below $80\%$ within ${\approx}10\mathrm{h}$ . In contrast, the device with P3HT/PVK heterointerface demonstrates sustained performance, maintaining an efficiency of $87.2\%$ even after $217\mathrm{~h~}$ , showing an enhanced operational stability compared to the PVK counterpart.
To verify the generalizability of the P3HT/PVK heterointerface in PSCs, planar devices with a structure of FTO/ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ /FAMAbased perovskite/spiro-OMeTAD/Au also were fabricated and tested; the results are shown in Figure $5\mathrm{d},\mathrm{e}$ , and Table S7 (Supporting Information). The highest PCE of P3HT/PVK device reaches $24.53\%$ with other enhanced photovoltaic parameters, as well as the certified PCE of $23.94\%$ by the National Photovoltaic Industry Metrology and Testing Center (NPVM, China, National Institute of Metrology in China (Figure S25, Supporting Information). Together with the results above, the feasibility of P3HT/PVK heterointerface is confirmed in the potential application of planar PSCs with different construction.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have constructed a perovskite-based heterointerface through spinodal decomposition and uncovered that the heterointerface has a significant impact on carrier dynamics. P3HT/PVK heterointerface can effectively relieve the energy barrier between HTL and perovskite and reduce the energy loss during the directional migration of carriers. The carrier loss can also be drastically mitigated since the P3HT/PVK heterointerface provides effective hole transport channels and reduce detrimental recombination. The PSC with P3HT/PVK heterointerface achieved a power conversion efficiency of $24.53\%$ (certified $23.94\%$ and retained $88.46\%$ of their initial efficiency after $960\mathrm{~h~}$ ; While the pristine device obtained an efficiency of $22.62\%$ and retained only $45.45\%$ after $960\mathrm{{h}}$ storage. Our findings open a pathway for highefficiency PSCs and another optoelectronics.
